                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Tunes t.param numbers by gradient descent. Build, from t.param and ordinary torch nodes, a measure of how wrong the result is - a loss, one number, smaller is better - and send it here. t.optimize traces it back to every t.param it was worked out from, finds which way each number should move to shrink it, and moves them a little. Repeat, and the numbers settle where the loss is smallest.\n\nNothing needs to be wired back to the t.param nodes except 'again'. After a step the numbers have changed, so the loss must be worked out afresh before the next step: 'again' asks for that. Wire it to the t.param nodes - to the ones on cold inlets first - or to whatever starts the calculation. However many steps are asked for at once, they run one after another, not nested.\n\nThe trail back from the loss must stay in torch: a node that turns it into a plain number, a list or a numpy array cuts it, and t.optimize says so. Most t. nodes keep it, as do + - * / and the quaternion conversions, quaternion_multiply and swing_twist.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.optimize <learning rate>\nDefault: learning rate 0.01.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nloss:\nThe loss. What happens next depends on 'step on'.\n\ntrain (checkbox, also an inlet):\nOn (the default), it learns. Off, it only shows the loss, and the t.param nodes stop keeping their trail - pure use of what was learned, at no extra cost.\n\nstep (button):\nIn 'bang' mode: ask for a fresh loss through 'again' and take the steps.\n\nreset (button):\nForgets the optimizer's momentum and the step count. The numbers are kept; t.param's own reset puts those back.\n\nstep on:\nAn option. each loss (the default): every loss that arrives is stepped on. bang: only the step button (or a bang) steps. every frame: it steps by itself every frame, asking for each loss through 'again'.\n\nsteps:\nAn option. Steps per loss, bang or frame: 1 by default. More learns faster per frame, at more work per frame.\n\noptimizer:\nAn option: adam (the default), adamw, sgd or rmsprop.\n\nlearning rate:\nAn option. How big a step is. Too small learns slowly; too big overshoots and jitters.\n\nclip gradient:\nAn option. Above 0, a step is never steeper than this, which steadies a loss with occasional wild frames.\n\npull to zero:\nAn option. Above 0, adds this much of the sum of the squared parameters to the loss, so numbers only grow where the loss really asks for it - a guard against a learned correction drifting where the loss cannot see.\n\nautosave every:\nAn option. Above 0, every this many steps the t.param nodes that have a path write their numbers.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nThe loss as a plain number, every time one arrives.\n\nsteps:\nHow many steps have been taken.\n\ngradient size:\nHow steeply the loss changes with the numbers - near 0 when they have settled.\n\nagain:\nA bang when a fresh loss is needed.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_points subpatcher makes 50 points on the line 3x + 0.5. Two t.param numbers, a slope and an offset, start at 0; the * and + nodes draw their line through the same x, and t.mse_loss measures how far it lies from the points. t.optimize steps every frame: the loss falls to 0 as the slope reaches 3 and the offset 0.5. Click reset on either t.param to watch it learn again.\n\nRELATED:\nt.param holds the numbers. t.mse_loss and t.l1_loss measure how wrong a result is. quaternion_multiply and swing_twist keep the trail through rotations.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1500,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Tunes t.param numbers by gradient descent. Build, from t.param and ordinary torch nodes, a measure of how wrong the result is - a loss, one number, smaller is better - and send it here. t.optimize traces it back to every t.param it was worked out from, finds which way each number should move to shrink it, and moves them a little. Repeat, and the numbers settle where the loss is smallest.\n\nNothing needs to be wired back to the t.param nodes except 'again'. After a step the numbers have changed, so the loss must be worked out afresh before the next step: 'again' asks for that. Wire it to the t.param nodes - to the ones on cold inlets first - or to whatever starts the calculation. However many steps are asked for at once, they run one after another, not nested.\n\nThe trail back from the loss must stay in torch: a node that turns it into a plain number, a list or a numpy array cuts it, and t.optimize says so. Most t. nodes keep it, as do + - * / and the quaternion conversions, quaternion_multiply and swing_twist.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.optimize <learning rate>\nDefault: learning rate 0.01.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nloss:\nThe loss. What happens next depends on 'step on'.\n\ntrain (checkbox, also an inlet):\nOn (the default), it learns. Off, it only shows the loss, and the t.param nodes stop keeping their trail - pure use of what was learned, at no extra cost.\n\nstep (button):\nIn 'bang' mode: ask for a fresh loss through 'again' and take the steps.\n\nreset (button):\nForgets the optimizer's momentum and the step count. The numbers are kept; t.param's own reset puts those back.\n\nstep on:\nAn option. each loss (the default): every loss that arrives is stepped on. bang: only the step button (or a bang) steps. every frame: it steps by itself every frame, asking for each loss through 'again'.\n\nsteps:\nAn option. Steps per loss, bang or frame: 1 by default. More learns faster per frame, at more work per frame.\n\noptimizer:\nAn option: adam (the default), adamw, sgd or rmsprop.\n\nlearning rate:\nAn option. How big a step is. Too small learns slowly; too big overshoots and jitters.\n\nclip gradient:\nAn option. Above 0, a step is never steeper than this, which steadies a loss with occasional wild frames.\n\npull to zero:\nAn option. Above 0, adds this much of the sum of the squared parameters to the loss, so numbers only grow where the loss really asks for it - a guard against a learned correction drifting where the loss cannot see.\n\nautosave every:\nAn option. Above 0, every this many steps the t.param nodes that have a path write their numbers.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nThe loss as a plain number, every time one arrives.\n\nsteps:\nHow many steps have been taken.\n\ngradient size:\nHow steeply the loss changes with the numbers - near 0 when they have settled.\n\nagain:\nA bang when a fresh loss is needed.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_points subpatcher makes 50 points on the line 3x + 0.5. Two t.param numbers, a slope and an offset, start at 0; the * and + nodes draw their line through the same x, and t.mse_loss measures how far it lies from the points. t.optimize steps every frame: the loss falls to 0 as the slope reaches 3 and the offset 0.5. Click reset on either t.param to watch it learn again.\n\nRELATED:\nt.param holds the numbers. t.mse_loss and t.l1_loss measure how wrong a result is. quaternion_multiply and swing_twist keep the trail through rotations."
                },
